# Normalise line endings in the index. Without this the repo's committed EOLs
# depended on whichever machine and editor wrote each file: README.md and
# scripts/run_parity.ps1 were stored with CRLF while every other text file was
# stored with LF (`git ls-files --eol`). A later edit from a different tool
# then rewrites every line of such a file, so a one-line change arrives as a
# whole-file diff and the real change is invisible in review.
#
# tests/... has no equivalent here (this repo's tests are .mjs), so the
# guard lives in scripts/eol_check.mjs and runs in CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touch, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary

# ...except this one, which is not an image. fixtures/screenshot.png is a plain
# text file carrying a fake token, and it wears the .png extension precisely so
# the corpus exercises the `**/*.png` exclude rule. Marking it `binary` would
# freeze whatever bytes the working tree happened to hold -- on Windows that is
# the CRLF-converted copy git produced back when it (correctly, for a text file)
# treated it as text, i.e. 210 bytes where the committed blob has 207.
fixtures/screenshot.png text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one place CRLF is conventional on the consuming side, but
# pwsh 7 reads LF fine on every platform and mixed EOLs cost more than they buy.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
